STRSPN(3)                      P32W Unix                       STRSPN(3)

NAME
       strspn, strcspn, strtok - split strings at delimiters

SYNOPSIS
       #include <string.h>

       size_t strspn(const char *s, const char *set);
       size_t strcspn(const char *s, const char *set);
       char *strtok(char *s, const char *delim);

DESCRIPTION
       strspn returns the length of the longest start of s made only
       of characters in the string set; strcspn, of characters not
       in set.

       strtok splits a string into tokens separated by runs of the
       characters of delim. The first call passes the string s;
       each later call passes NULL to go on through the same string
       (delim may differ from call to call). Each call skips
       delimiters, writes a NUL over the delimiter after the token,
       and returns the token. s is changed.

RETURN VALUE
       strspn and strcspn return a length. strtok returns the next
       token, or NULL when none is left.

EXAMPLES
       char line[] = "root:x:0:0";
       char *f;
       for (f = strtok(line, ":"); f; f = strtok(NULL, ":"))
           printf("%s\n", f);

SEE ALSO
       string(3), strchr(3)

BUGS
       strtok keeps its place in a static variable: it cannot work
       on two strings at once, and there is no strtok_r. Empty
       fields (as in "a::b") are skipped, not returned, so strtok is
       no use for files such as /etc/passwd whose fields may be
       empty.
